diagnosis_text:
A thorough 15-minute investigation of every layer of the dc_clos_bgp fabric finds no active fault. All five FRR routers (leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, super_spine_router_0) have had their BGP daemons running continuously since lab startup with exactly one startup cycle each (confirmed by "Configuration Read" counts in the FRR logs). Every BGP session across the fabric is in the Established state, has been up since the lab started, and shows zero connections dropped. Keepalives are being exchanged at the expected ~60-second interval with no gaps or hold-timer expirations anywhere in the log history. All interfaces on leaf_router_0_0 (eth0, eth1, eth2) are UP with zero errors and zero drops. Routing tables are correct: leaf_router_0_0 holds 10.0.0.0/24 as a connected route and 10.0.1.0/24 via BGP from both spines. End-to-end pings from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) complete with 0% packet loss and sub-2 ms RTT. The one unusual configuration present on all routers — `debug bgp keepalives` and `debug bgp updates in/out` — generates roughly seven log lines per minute total and has produced a 12 KB log file on a 933 GB filesystem; it is having no measurable effect on protocol timing or stability. The witness report that leaf_router_0_0 "keeps restarting itself" is directly contradicted by the FRR log and process table. The reported symptom — connection going up and down — cannot be reproduced and is not supported by any evidence in the current lab state. No corrective action is required; the fabric is operating correctly.
=== END ===